UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 25, Lesson 1: Game play and fair play', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Game play and fair play mean following rules, respecting opponents and accepting results without unkind behaviour.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nList three fair-play actions after a close loss.\n\nFEEDBACK\nCongratulate; no blame; thank helpers — any three.\n\nWATCH FOR\nWinning by cheating is not success.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nPlay a game with a fair-play checklist scored by a peer marshal. Reflect.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"Game play and fair play mean following rules, respecting opponents and accepting results without unkind behaviour.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Learn and discuss","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PH2-MSP-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Play a game with a fair-play checklist scored by a peer marshal. Reflect. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using fair play on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Winning by cheating is not success.. Correction: Congratulate; no blame; thank helpers — any three.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Winning by cheating is not success. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating fair play on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with fair play, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about fair play with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for fair play. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for fair play. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Winning by cheating is not success","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","Congratulate; no blame; thank helpers — any three"],"question":"List three fair-play actions after a close loss. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for fair play; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Careful use of rule and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Winning by cheating is not success","Game play and fair play mean following rules, respecting opponents and accepting results without unkind behaviour","Congratulate; no blame; thank helpers — any three"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with fair play?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named rule and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Winning by cheating is not success","Name rule and explain why it matters only, without linking it to fair play","Apply fair play to new material and name the rule and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence"],"question":"Which response best applies fair play with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses safe, healthy or respectful choices appropriately for this lesson on fair play. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Congratulate; no blame; thank helpers — any three","Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety","Use a put-down and call it honesty","Winning by cheating is not success"],"question":"Which response best uses safe, healthy or respectful choices for fair play?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use a put-down and call it honesty","Winning by cheating is not success","Name a body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy and say how it helps with fair play","Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about fair play?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (Game play and fair play mean following rules, respecting opponents and) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Example: shake hands/high-five","Winning by cheating is not success","Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety","Use a put-down and call it honesty"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about fair play?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Check safety and fairness, include others, and explain the healthier choice"],"question":"Which action keeps safe, healthy or respectful choices accurate for fair play?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Play a game with a fair-play checklist scored by a peer marshal","Retype the worked example with no new example","Winning by cheating is not success","Name rule and explain why it matters only, with no linked example"],"question":"Which independent response best applies fair play to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about fair play with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating fair play on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using fair play on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for fair play. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with fair play, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Winning by cheating is not success.. Correction: Congratulate; no blame; thank helpers — any three.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y4-w25-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise fair play.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Movement Skill and Performance","success_criteria":["I can use fair play with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify rule and explain why it matters.","I can practise respect independently on a new prompt.","I can notice win needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Game play and fair play mean following rules, respecting opponents and accepting results without unkind behaviour.","options":["A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no reason","Game play and fair play mean following rules, respecting opponents and accepting results without unkind behaviour"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about fair play with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: shake hands/high-five; no sledging; thank umpires/helpers.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList three fair-play actions after a close loss.\n\nFEEDBACK\nCongratulate; no blame; thank helpers — any three.","explain":"Game play and fair play mean following rules, respecting opponents and accepting results without unkind behaviour.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Winning by cheating is not success. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Fair play in games.","caption":"Cheating ≠ success.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Fair play</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">rules respect</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Accept</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">results kindly</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: shake hands/high-five; no sledging; thank umpires/helpers. Then write your own reasons.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.\nJuno: Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: shake hands/high-five; no sledging; thank umpires/helpers. Then write your own reasons."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.","options":["A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about fair play with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: shake hands/high-five; no sledging; thank umpires/helpers. Look at each cue, rule or choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does for safety, health or relationships.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","explain":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Show the reasoning","visual":[{"alt":"Fair-play check.","caption":"Three actions.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Checklist game</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">reflect</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">sledge after loss</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison.","options":["A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no reason","Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about fair play with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nWinning by cheating is not success. Here is a more helpful example: Example: shake hands/high-five; no sledging; thank umpires/helpers.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder or healthier choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nCongratulate; no blame; thank helpers — any three.","explain":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Winning by cheating is not success. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example","A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about fair play with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nGame play and fair play mean following rules, respecting opponents and accepting results without unkind behaviour. Now choose a different situation to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","explain":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Transfer to a new situation"}],"title":"Year 4, Week 25, Lesson 1: Game play and fair play","unit_number":5,"unit_title":"Net, court and fielding games","week":25,"week_in_unit":5,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: shake hands/high-five; no sledging; thank umpires/helpers.\n\nWrite what makes this example safe, healthy or respectful, naming the cues, rules or choices used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Fair play in games.","caption":"Cheating ≠ success.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Fair play</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">rules respect</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Accept</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">results kindly</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Fair-play check.","caption":"Three actions.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Checklist game</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">reflect</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">sledge after los